NNAAMMEE
     ppwwdd__mmkkddbb — generate the password databases

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppwwdd__mmkkddbb [--cc] [--pp | --ss] [--dd _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y] [--uu _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e] _f_i_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ppwwdd__mmkkddbb creates a pair of Berkeley databases from _f_i_l_e and installs them
     into _/_e_t_c_/_s_p_w_d_._d_b and _/_e_t_c_/_p_w_d_._d_b.  The _f_i_l_e argument is renamed to
     _/_e_t_c_/_m_a_s_t_e_r_._p_a_s_s_w_d.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc      Check whether _f_i_l_e is in the correct format.  Do not change, add,
             or remove any files.

     --dd _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Operate in a base directory other than the default of _/_e_t_c.  All
             absolute paths (including _f_i_l_e) will be made relative to
             _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y.  Any directories specified as a part of _f_i_l_e will be
             stripped off.  This option is used to create password databases
             in directories other than _/_e_t_c; for instance in a chroot(8) jail.

     --pp      Also create a legacy password file and install it into
             _/_e_t_c_/_p_a_s_s_w_d.

     --ss      Only update the secure version of the database.  This is most
             commonly used in conjunction with the --uu flag during a password
             change.  Because the insecure database doesn't contain the pass‐
             word, there is no reason to update it if the only change is in
             the password field.  Cannot be used in conjunction with the --pp
             flag.

     --uu _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e
             Only update the record for the specified user.  Utilities that
             operate on a single user can use this option to avoid the over‐
             head of rebuilding the entire database.  This option must never
             be used if the line number of the user's record in
             _/_e_t_c_/_m_a_s_t_e_r_._p_a_s_s_w_d has changed.

     _f_i_l_e    The absolute path to a file in master.passwd(5) format.

     The two databases differ in that the secure version contains the user's
     encrypted password and the insecure version has an asterisk (‘*’).

     The databases are used by the C library password routines (see
     getpwent(3)).

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/master.passwd  current password file
     /etc/passwd         legacy password file
     /etc/pwd.db         insecure password database file
     /etc/pwd.db.tmp     temporary file
     /etc/spwd.db        secure password database file
     /etc/spwd.db.tmp    temporary file

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ppwwdd__mmkkddbb utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     chpass(1), passwd(1), dbopen(3), getpwent(3), passwd(5), vipw(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ppwwdd__mmkkddbb utility first appeared in 4.3BSD Net/2.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Keith Bostic

BBUUGGSS
     Because of the necessity for atomic update of the password files,
     ppwwdd__mmkkddbb uses rename(2) to install them.  This, however, requires that
     the file specified on the command line live on the same file system as
     the _/_e_t_c directory.

     There are the obvious races with multiple people running ppwwdd__mmkkddbb on dif‐
     ferent password files at the same time.  The front-ends to ppwwdd__mmkkddbb,
     chpass(1), passwd(1), and vipw(8) handle the locking necessary to avoid
     this problem.

